      "page_title": "Drop Order Rules",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Tuple, array and `Vec` elements drop in index order",
            "An enum drops the active variant's fields in declaration order",
            "Function parameters drop in reverse, like locals"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "The Trap",
          "text": "Locals reverse, fields forward. Assuming struct fields also reverse is the single most common drop-order bug."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Forcing An Early Death"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "rust",
          "lines": [
            "{ let g = m.lock(); } // guard freed at }",
            "drop(x); // moved into a fn that ends"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "A bare `{ }` block releases a lock guard early",
            "Nested scopes drop entirely at the inner `}`",
            "Temporaries in one statement drop in reverse creation order"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Where A Moved Value Dies"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "At the new owner's scope end, not the old binding's",
            "`let` position starts a sub-scope for later statements",
            "Declaration position is what controls lifetime"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "rust",
          "lines": [
            "let s = String::from(\"a\");",
            "let s = String::from(\"b\"); // shadows only",
            "// the first s still drops at end of block"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Drop Flags",
          "items": [
            "Conditional moves get a hidden stack flag",
            "Set only when the compiler can't prove statically",
            "Read at scope exit to decide: drop or not"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Do Not Trust Exit",
          "text": "Static and thread-local destructors are not guaranteed to run at process exit. Flush your buffers explicitly."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Vec Owns Its Elements",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
